# Where the Rust weapon catalog comes from

Everything here was read on **2026-10-21**. `catalog.json` is the single-kind
form (`kind: "weapons"`, Warzone's shape). The game is **Rust Console Edition**
(PlayStation and Xbox), because the EXO is a console adapter; where it differs
from Rust on PC is said below.

**Two publishers.** Facepunch Studios makes Rust. Rust Console Edition is
published and developed by **Double Eleven** with Facepunch, and its official
site is [rust.double11.com](https://rust.double11.com/), whose
[news page](https://rust.double11.com/news) holds every console patch note and
dev blog since 2021 (164 posts, all read through the site's own JSON feed,
`https://rust.double11.com/news?format=json`, on 2026-10-21).

**Pictures: publisher artwork only (decision D116).** Every picture in
`weapons/` is Facepunch's own inventory icon for the item, downloaded from
Facepunch's file server `files.facepunch.com` at
`https://files.facepunch.com/rust/item/<item shortname>_512.png`. That is the
picture Facepunch's own wiki shows on each item's page (for example
[wiki.facepunch.com/rust/item/rifle.ak](https://wiki.facepunch.com/rust/item/rifle.ak)
embeds `https://files.facepunch.com/rust/item/rifle.ak_512.png`). Each 512 x
512 RGBA icon was cropped to the weapon (transparent border removed, 4 px kept)
and scaled to fit 480 x 300, transparency kept. None came from a fan wiki,
Fandom, RustLabs, a loadout site or an image search, and none is generated.
They are Facepunch's copyright, used in the app to identify the weapon, the
same risk the owner accepted in D116. Delete a `.png` and set that weapon's
`image` to `null` and the app shows the class symbol instead.

The icons are the PC game's icons. Rust Console Edition uses the same item
art (Double Eleven's notes repeatedly bring console models and effects in line
"to match the Steam version"), but no console page shows an icon, so this is
not confirmed for every item.

## What is in Rust Console Edition today

- **Current update: 1.16**, live since 2026-09-23
  ([Dev Blog #55 - 1.16 Patch Notes](https://rust.double11.com/news/dev-blog-55)).
  Neither 1.16 nor [Halloween 2026](https://rust.double11.com/news/halloween-2026)
  (2026-10-15 to 2026-11-05) names a new weapon. The Halloween post says "Grab
  a stake" and mentions limited-time gear; Facepunch's PC Weapons category has
  a Vampire Stake, but no console page names it, so it is not in the catalog.
- Rust has **no seasons**: the catalog's `season` is the update live on the
  day it was checked.
- **57 weapons in 12 groups, 56 with a Facepunch picture.**

### Names

Names are the game's own item names, as Facepunch's wiki prints them on each
item's page (`https://wiki.facepunch.com/rust/item/<shortname>`; the item
pages are Facepunch's, generated from the game's item data: description, item
ID, craft cost). Every item's `sources` lists that page and the Double Eleven
console posts that name it. Where the two spell it differently, the catalog
uses Facepunch's item name and keeps Double Eleven's as an alias:

| console notes say | catalog uses | alias kept |
|---|---|---|
| Nail Gun / Nail gun | Nailgun | Nail Gun |
| Handmade LMG (Patch Notes 1.90) | HMLMG (and later console notes) | Handmade LMG |
| MP5 | MP5A4 | MP5 |
| Military Flame Thrower | Military Flame Thrower (no Facepunch page) | Military Flamethrower |
| Flamethrower | Flame Thrower | Flamethrower |

Two weapons have **no Facepunch wiki page**, so their names come from Double
Eleven's console notes alone: **Blunderbuss** and **Military Flame Thrower**.

